@lang en

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- The Interpretation Act 1978 (1978 c. 30): the rules that bear on time.
--
-- Source: the plain-text rendering in ../../registers/source-bundle/ukpga-1978-30.txt, made by a converter script from
-- https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1978/30/data.xml (version 2026-01-01). It is not the official text.
-- Licence: see SOURCE-LICENSE.md and the subject-level note it points at.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE ANSWERS, from the text alone:
--   s 4    when an Act, or a provision of an Act, comes into force: the day, and the moment within it
--          ("at the beginning of that day"); and, through s 23(1) and Sch 2 para 6, when subordinate
--          legislation that names a day comes into force;
--   s 9    the time standard of a time referred to in an Act (Greenwich mean time), subject to the Summer Time Act 1972;
--   s 13   when an instrument may be made under an Act that is not yet in force;
--   Sch 1  "month" (calendar month), "financial year" (the twelve months ending with 31st March), "commencement".
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE REFUSES, because the text is silent (each is a named REFUSE; see NOTES.md, fork register F1 to F4):
--   * how a period of days or weeks is counted from a day: the Act has no rule, and does not define "day" or "week";
--   * how a period of months or years is counted after an event: the Act says only that a month is a calendar month,
--     and does not define "year";
--   * what happens when the last day of a period is a day of rest, recess or holiday: the Act has no such rule;
--   * when subordinate legislation that says nothing about its coming into force comes into force: s 23(1) excludes s 4(b).
--
-- Days of rest, recess and holiday are an INPUT, never computed here, and no holiday calendar is built in.
-- No answer is borrowed from the common law, from another jurisdiction, or from daydate's `add months`.

§ `Interpretation Act 1978 — the rules that bear on time`

§§ `Nouns of this module`

DECLARE `Kind of enactment` IS ONE OF
    `an Act`
    `subordinate legislation`

DECLARE `Provision on coming into force` IS ONE OF
    `a particular day`       HAS `that day`            IS A DATE
    `a day to be fixed later` HAS `the day once fixed` IS A MAYBE DATE
    `no provision`

-- For an Act, `the day it was passed or made` is the day on which it receives the Royal Assent (s 4(b)).
DECLARE `An enactment` HAS
    `its kind`                            IS A `Kind of enactment`
    `the day it was passed or made`       IS A DATE
    `what it says about coming into force` IS A `Provision on coming into force`

-- The text's own words for the moment: "at the beginning of that day" (s 4(a), (b)).
DECLARE `Moment within a day` IS ONE OF
    `the beginning of the day`

DECLARE `Coming into force` HAS
    `the day`                   IS A DATE
    `the moment within the day` IS A `Moment within a day`

DECLARE `Time standard` IS ONE OF
    `Greenwich mean time`

DECLARE `Clock reading` HAS
    `hour`     IS A NUMBER
    `minute`   IS A NUMBER
    `second`   IS A NUMBER
    `standard` IS A `Time standard`

DECLARE `A period of days` HAS
    `first day` IS A DATE
    `last day`  IS A DATE

