§§ `Section 26 and section 22(1): the commencement of the Act itself`

-- src:216 | 26 Commencement.
-- src:217 | This Act shall come into force on 1st January 1979.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 26 (src:216-217)
GIVETH A DATE
`the day on which the Interpretation Act 1978 comes into force` MEANS YMD 1979 1 1

-- src:103 | (1) This Act applies to itself, to any Act passed after the commencement of this Act (subject , in the case of section 20(2A) to (6), to the provision made in section 20(2A) or (3) and, in the case of section 20A, to the provision made in that section) and, to the extent specified in Part I of Schedule 2, to Acts passed before the commencement of this Act.
-- src:107 | (1) The provisions of this Act, except sections 1 to 3 and 4(b), apply, so far as applicable and unless the contrary intention appears, to subordinate legislation made after the commencement of this Act and, to the extent specified in Part II of Schedule 2, to subordinate legislation made before the commencement of this Act, as they apply to Acts.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 22(1) (src:103); s 23(1) (src:107)
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the day is on or after the commencement of the Interpretation Act 1978` `the day` MEANS
    `the day` AT LEAST `the day on which the Interpretation Act 1978 comes into force`

§§ `Section 4: time of commencement`

-- Whether section 4(a) reaches an enactment. Two routes in, one for each kind.
--   An Act:  passed after the commencement of this Act (s 22(1)), or passed before it and after the year 1889 (Sch 2 para 3).
--   Subordinate legislation:  made after the commencement (s 23(1), which excepts only ss 1 to 3 and 4(b)),
--            or made before it, in which case s 4(a) applies "as they apply to Acts passed at that time" (Sch 2 para 6, para 3).
-- "after the commencement" is read as: on or after the day the Act commences, since it commences at the beginning of that day
-- (fork F9 in NOTES.md).

-- src:402 | 3 The following apply to Acts passed after the year 1889:—
-- src:403 | Section 4
-- src:428 | Part II — Subordinate Legislation
-- src:429 | Sections 4(a), 9 and 19(1), and so much of Schedule 1 as defines the following expressions, namely—
-- src:436 | apply to subordinate legislation made at any time before the commencement of this Act as they apply to Acts passed at that time.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 22(1) (src:103); s 23(1) (src:107); Sch 2 para 3 (src:402-403); Sch 2 para 6 (src:428-429, 436)
GIVEN e IS AN `An enactment`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 4(a) applies to` e MEANS
    CONSIDER e's `its kind`
    WHEN `an Act` THEN
            `the day is on or after the commencement of the Interpretation Act 1978` (e's `the day it was passed or made`)
        OR  DATE_YEAR (e's `the day it was passed or made`) GREATER THAN 1889
    WHEN `subordinate legislation` THEN
            `the day is on or after the commencement of the Interpretation Act 1978` (e's `the day it was passed or made`)
        OR  DATE_YEAR (e's `the day it was passed or made`) GREATER THAN 1889

-- src:402 | 3 The following apply to Acts passed after the year 1889:—
-- src:403 | Section 4
-- src:107 | (1) The provisions of this Act, except sections 1 to 3 and 4(b), apply, so far as applicable and unless the contrary intention appears, to subordinate legislation made after the commencement of this Act and, to the extent specified in Part II of Schedule 2, to subordinate legislation made before the commencement of this Act, as they apply to Acts.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 22(1) (src:103); Sch 2 para 3 (src:402-403); s 23(1) (src:107): 4(b) is excepted for subordinate legislation, and Sch 2 Part II lists only 4(a)
GIVEN e IS AN `An enactment`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 4(b) applies to` e MEANS
    CONSIDER e's `its kind`
    WHEN `an Act` THEN
            `the day is on or after the commencement of the Interpretation Act 1978` (e's `the day it was passed or made`)
        OR  DATE_YEAR (e's `the day it was passed or made`) GREATER THAN 1889
    WHEN `subordinate legislation` THEN FALSE

-- The moment is, in both limbs, "at the beginning of" a day: the text's own words, kept as a value.
-- Limb (a) takes the particular day the enactment provides for. A day "to be fixed later", once it has been fixed,
-- is read as a particular day provided for (fork F5); until it is fixed neither limb gives a day.
-- Limb (b) takes "the day on which the Act receives the Royal Assent", which is `the day it was passed or made` of an Act.

-- src:12 | 4 Time of commencement.
-- src:13 | An Act or provision of an Act comes into force—
-- src:14 | (a) where provision is made for it to come into force on a particular day, at the beginning of that day;
-- src:15 | (b) where no provision is made for its coming into force, at the beginning of the day on which the Act receives the Royal Assent.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 4 (src:12-15)
GIVEN e IS AN `An enactment`
GIVETH A `Coming into force`
`section 4 — when the enactment comes into force` e MEANS
    CONSIDER e's `what it says about coming into force`
    WHEN `a particular day` d THEN
        IF   `section 4(a) applies to` e
        THEN `Coming into force` WITH
                 `the day`                   IS d
                 `the moment within the day` IS `the beginning of the day`
        ELSE `section 4 does not apply to an enactment passed or made in or before 1889, and the Interpretation Act 1978 has no other rule on its coming into force`
    WHEN `a day to be fixed later` fixed THEN
        CONSIDER fixed
        WHEN JUST d THEN
            IF   `section 4(a) applies to` e
            THEN `Coming into force` WITH
                     `the day`                   IS d
                     `the moment within the day` IS `the beginning of the day`
            ELSE `section 4 does not apply to an enactment passed or made in or before 1889, and the Interpretation Act 1978 has no other rule on its coming into force`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN
            `the enactment provides for a day to be fixed later and none is fixed yet, so neither limb of section 4 gives a day`
    WHEN `no provision` THEN
        IF   `section 4(b) applies to` e
        THEN `Coming into force` WITH
                 `the day`                   IS e's `the day it was passed or made`
                 `the moment within the day` IS `the beginning of the day`
        ELSE CONSIDER e's `its kind`
             WHEN `an Act` THEN
                 `section 4 does not apply to an enactment passed or made in or before 1889, and the Interpretation Act 1978 has no other rule on its coming into force`
             WHEN `subordinate legislation` THEN
                 `the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule on when subordinate legislation that makes no provision comes into force`

-- src:244 | “Commencement”, in relation to an Act or enactment, means the time when the Act or enactment comes into force.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 Sch 1 "Commencement" (src:244); s 5
GIVEN e IS AN `An enactment`
GIVETH A `Coming into force`
`Schedule 1 — the commencement of the enactment` e MEANS
    `section 4 — when the enactment comes into force` e

§§ `Section 9: references to time of day`

-- Reading A of fork F6: "the beginning of that day" in s 4 is an "expression of time" in an Act, so s 9 gives its standard.
-- Reading B: it is a boundary between days, not a point on a clock, and s 9 does not reach it.
-- The commencement function above answers in the text's own words and takes neither reading.
-- The functions below answer on reading A, and refuse where s 9 itself is displaced or subject to the Summer Time Act 1972.

-- src:28 | 9 References to time of day.
-- src:29 | Subject to section 3 of the Summer Time Act 1972 (construction of references to points of time during the period of summer time), whenever an expression of time occurs in an Act, the time referred to shall, unless it is otherwise specifically stated, be held to be Greenwich mean time.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 9 (src:28-29)
GIVEN `the time is otherwise specifically stated` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the time falls in a period of summer time` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Time standard`
`section 9 — the time standard of an expression of time in an Act` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the time falls in a period of summer time`
                THEN `section 9 is subject to section 3 of the Summer Time Act 1972, which is not among the sources, and the time falls in a period of summer time`
           IF `the time is otherwise specifically stated`
                THEN `the time is otherwise specifically stated, so section 9 does not say what standard it is in`
           OTHERWISE `Greenwich mean time`

@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 4 (src:14-15) read with s 9 (src:29), reading A of fork F6
GIVEN moment IS A `Moment within a day`
      `the time falls in a period of summer time` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Clock reading`
`section 4 read with section 9 — the clock reading of the moment` moment `the time falls in a period of summer time` MEANS
    CONSIDER moment
    WHEN `the beginning of the day` THEN
        `Clock reading` WITH
            `hour`     IS 0
            `minute`   IS 0
            `second`   IS 0
            `standard` IS `section 9 — the time standard of an expression of time in an Act` FALSE `the time falls in a period of summer time`

§§ `Section 13: anticipatory exercise of powers`

-- s 13 is a permission. Where it does not give one, it does not forbid: so the functions answer "s 13 permits", and FALSE
-- means "s 13 does not give the permission", never "the power may not be exercised".

DECLARE `An anticipatory power` HAS
    `the day the Act was passed`                                   IS A DATE
    `the Act or provision does not come into force immediately on its passing` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the power is to make subordinate legislation, make appointments, give notices, prescribe forms or do any other thing for the purposes of the Act` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is necessary or expedient for bringing the Act or a provision into force, or for giving full effect to it at or after the time it comes into force` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the contrary intention appears`                               IS A BOOLEAN

-- The conditions common to both limbs of s 13.
-- src:38 | 13 Anticipatory exercise of powers.
-- src:39 | Where an Act which (or any provision of which) does not come into force immediately on its passing confers power to make subordinate legislation, or to make appointments, give notices, prescribe forms or do any other thing for the purposes of the Act, then, unless the contrary intention appears, the power may be exercised, and any instrument made thereunder may be made so as to come into force, at any time after the passing of the Act so far as may be necessary or expedient for the purpose—
-- src:40 | (a) of bringing the Act or any provision of the Act into force; or
-- src:41 | (b) of giving full effect to the Act or any such provision at or after the time when it comes into force.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 13 (src:38-41)
GIVEN power IS AN `An anticipatory power`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the conditions of section 13 are met by` power MEANS
        power's `the Act or provision does not come into force immediately on its passing`
    AND power's `the power is to make subordinate legislation, make appointments, give notices, prescribe forms or do any other thing for the purposes of the Act`
    AND power's `it is necessary or expedient for bringing the Act or a provision into force, or for giving full effect to it at or after the time it comes into force`
    AND NOT power's `the contrary intention appears`

-- "any instrument made thereunder may be made so as to come into force ... at any time after the passing of the Act".
-- An instrument that comes into force at the beginning of a day (s 4(a)) comes into force before any moment of the
-- day of passing, so only a later day is after the passing (fork F8).
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 13 (src:38-41) read with s 4(a) (src:14)
GIVEN power IS AN `An anticipatory power`
      `the instrument comes into force` IS A `Coming into force`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 13 permits an instrument made under the Act to come into force at` power `the instrument comes into force` MEANS
        `the conditions of section 13 are met by` power
    AND CONSIDER `the instrument comes into force`'s `the moment within the day`
        WHEN `the beginning of the day` THEN
            `the instrument comes into force`'s `the day` GREATER THAN power's `the day the Act was passed`

-- "the power may be exercised ... at any time after the passing of the Act": the day of exercise is compared with the day
-- of passing. A later day is after the passing. The same day may or may not be, and the input carries no moment within the
-- day, so the function refuses for it.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 13 (src:38-41)
GIVEN power IS AN `An anticipatory power`
      `the day the power is exercised` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 13 permits the power to be exercised on` power `the day the power is exercised` MEANS
    IF   NOT `the conditions of section 13 are met by` power
    THEN FALSE
    ELSE BRANCH IF `the day the power is exercised` GREATER THAN power's `the day the Act was passed` THEN TRUE
                IF `the day the power is exercised` EQUALS power's `the day the Act was passed`
                    THEN `section 13 says after the passing, and a day alone does not say whether a moment on the day of passing is after it`
                OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `Schedule 1 and Schedule 2 para 4: whether a defined word reaches an enactment`

-- Schedule 1 paragraphs carry a year or a date in brackets. Sch 2 para 4(1)(a): such a paragraph applies to Acts passed
-- "on or after the date, or after the year, so specified". A year is therefore a bar of "after that year".
-- An Act passed after the commencement of this Act takes Schedule 1 whole (s 22(1)); subordinate legislation made after it
-- takes it as it applies to Acts (s 23(1)). Subordinate legislation made before it takes only the expressions Sch 2 para 6
-- lists, and "month" and "financial year" are not among them.

-- src:413 | 4 (1) Subject to the following provisions of this paragraph—
-- src:414 | (a) paragraphs of Schedule 1 at the end of which a year or date . . . is specified or described apply, so far as applicable, to Acts passed on or after the date, or after the year, so specified or described: and
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 Sch 2 para 4(1)(a) (src:413-414); s 22(1) (src:103); s 23(1) (src:107); Sch 2 para 6 (src:429, 436)
GIVEN e IS AN `An enactment`
      `the year in brackets` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a Schedule 1 paragraph tagged with a year applies to` e `the year in brackets` MEANS
    CONSIDER e's `its kind`
    WHEN `an Act` THEN
            `the day is on or after the commencement of the Interpretation Act 1978` (e's `the day it was passed or made`)
        OR  DATE_YEAR (e's `the day it was passed or made`) GREATER THAN `the year in brackets`
    WHEN `subordinate legislation` THEN
        `the day is on or after the commencement of the Interpretation Act 1978` (e's `the day it was passed or made`)

§§ `Schedule 1: the time words the Act defines`

-- src:288 | “Month” means calendar month. [1850]
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 Sch 1 "Month" (src:288); s 5
GIVEN e IS AN `An enactment`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Schedule 1 defines month as a calendar month for` e MEANS
    `a Schedule 1 paragraph tagged with a year applies to` e 1850

DECLARE `Meaning of month` IS ONE OF
    `a calendar month`

@ref Interpretation Act 1978 Sch 1 "Month" (src:288)
GIVEN e IS AN `An enactment`
GIVETH A `Meaning of month`
`Schedule 1 — the meaning of month in` e MEANS
    IF   `Schedule 1 defines month as a calendar month for` e
    THEN `a calendar month`
    ELSE `Schedule 1 does not define month for this enactment`

-- "the twelve months ending with 31st March": the twelve calendar months that end with the month of March, so from the
-- 1st of April of the year before to the 31st of March (fork F7).
-- src:268 | “Financial year” means, in relation to matters relating to the Consolidated Fund, the National Loans Fund, or moneys provided by Parliament, or to the Exchequer or to central taxes or finance, the twelve months ending with 31st March. [1889]
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 Sch 1 "Financial year" (src:268)
GIVEN e IS AN `An enactment`
      `the matter relates to the Consolidated Fund, the National Loans Fund, moneys provided by Parliament, the Exchequer, or central taxes or finance` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the year in which it ends` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `A period of days`
`Schedule 1 — the financial year` e `the matter relates to the Consolidated Fund, the National Loans Fund, moneys provided by Parliament, the Exchequer, or central taxes or finance` `the year in which it ends` MEANS
    IF   NOT `the matter relates to the Consolidated Fund, the National Loans Fund, moneys provided by Parliament, the Exchequer, or central taxes or finance`
    THEN `Schedule 1 defines financial year only in relation to the Consolidated Fund, the National Loans Fund, moneys provided by Parliament, the Exchequer, or central taxes or finance`
    ELSE IF   `a Schedule 1 paragraph tagged with a year applies to` e 1889
         THEN `A period of days` WITH
                  `first day` IS YMD (`the year in which it ends` MINUS 1) 4 1
                  `last day`  IS YMD `the year in which it ends` 3 31
         ELSE `Schedule 1 does not define financial year for this enactment`

@ref Interpretation Act 1978 Sch 1 "Financial year" (src:268): the Act does not define the term for this enactment
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Schedule 1 does not define financial year for this enactment` MEANS
    REFUSE "Schedule 1 does not define financial year for this enactment"

§§ `Schedule 1: the time words, in the text's own words`

DECLARE `Time word` IS ONE OF
    `the word day`
    `the word week`
    `the word month`
    `the word year`
    `the phrase financial year`

@ref Interpretation Act 1978: "day", "week" and "year" are not defined (Sch 1 has no entry for any of them; see NOTES.md)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define day, week or year` MEANS
    REFUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define day, week or year"

-- src:268 | “Financial year” means, in relation to matters relating to the Consolidated Fund, the National Loans Fund, or moneys provided by Parliament, or to the Exchequer or to central taxes or finance, the twelve months ending with 31st March. [1889]
-- src:288 | “Month” means calendar month. [1850]
-- The answer is the text's own words, as a string. The "financial year" meaning is limited to the matters the entry names, and the
-- year and date marks are applied by `Schedule 1 defines month as a calendar month for` and its sisters, so this function does not.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 Sch 1 "Financial year" (src:268) and "Month" (src:288)
GIVEN word IS A `Time word`
GIVETH A STRING
`Schedule 1 — the meaning of the time word, in the text's own words` word MEANS
    CONSIDER word
    WHEN `the word day`   THEN `the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define day, week or year`
    WHEN `the word week`  THEN `the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define day, week or year`
    WHEN `the word month` THEN "calendar month"
    WHEN `the word year`  THEN `the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define day, week or year`
    WHEN `the phrase financial year` THEN "the twelve months ending with 31st March"

§§ `The periods of days, weeks, months and years: what the Act says, which is nothing`

-- src:288 | “Month” means calendar month. [1850]
-- A period of N days, weeks, months or years, counted from or after a day, is not given an end by any section.
-- The brief's six fork questions are answered in NOTES.md (F1 to F4); here each function refuses by name.

@ref Interpretation Act 1978: silent (see the refusals above)
GIVEN `the number of days` IS A NUMBER
      `the day it is counted from` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of a period of days counted from a day` `the number of days` `the day it is counted from` MEANS
    `the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day`

@ref Interpretation Act 1978: silent (see the refusals above)
GIVEN `the number of weeks` IS A NUMBER
      `the day it is counted from` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of a period of weeks counted from a day` `the number of weeks` `the day it is counted from` MEANS
    `the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule for counting a period of weeks from a day`

@ref Interpretation Act 1978 Sch 1 "Month" (src:288): a month is a calendar month; nothing says how a period of them is counted
GIVEN `the number of months` IS A NUMBER
      `the day of the event` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of a period of months counted after an event` `the number of months` `the day of the event` MEANS
    `the Interpretation Act 1978 defines a month as a calendar month but has no rule for counting a period of months after an event`

@ref Interpretation Act 1978: silent (see the refusals above)
GIVEN `the number of years` IS A NUMBER
      `the day of the event` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of a period of years counted after an event` `the number of years` `the day of the event` MEANS
    `the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define year and has no rule for counting a period of years after an event`

-- Days of rest, recess and holiday are an input: a function the caller supplies, from a day to whether it is one.
-- The Act has no rule that moves a last day off such a day, so the answer does not depend on the input.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978: silent (see the refusals above)
GIVEN `the last day reached by counting` IS A DATE
      `is a day of rest, recess or holiday` IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of a period once days of rest, recess and holidays are considered` `the last day reached by counting` `is a day of rest, recess or holiday` MEANS
    `the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule on a period whose last day is a day of rest, recess or holiday`

-- Whether a day falls inside a period that is counted from a day: it needs the last day, which no section gives.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978: silent (see the refusals above)
GIVEN `the number of days` IS A NUMBER
      `the day it is counted from` IS A DATE
      `the day asked about` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the day falls inside a period of days counted from a day` `the number of days` `the day it is counted from` `the day asked about` MEANS
    `the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day`

@ref Interpretation Act 1978: silent (see the refusals above)
GIVEN `the number of months` IS A NUMBER
      `the day of the event` IS A DATE
      `the day asked about` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the day falls inside a period of months counted after an event` `the number of months` `the day of the event` `the day asked about` MEANS
    `the Interpretation Act 1978 defines a month as a calendar month but has no rule for counting a period of months after an event`

-- NOT A RULE OF THE ACT. A closed interval of days whose first and last day the caller supplies. The Act does not say
-- whether the boundary days of a period are in it, so this takes the words "first day" and "last day" at face value:
-- both are in the period.
GIVEN `the day asked about` IS A DATE
      period IS A `A period of days`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the day falls inside the period` `the day asked about` period MEANS
        `the day asked about` AT LEAST period's `first day`
    AND `the day asked about` AT MOST  period's `last day`
